{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And the Egyptians will hear that You brought them up by Your power</b> — And the Egyptians will hear that You strike them: <b>that You brought them up</b>, for its meaning is in the language of אֲשֶׁר (that), and they saw that You brought them up by Your power from among them. And when they hear that You are killing them, they will not say that they sinned against You, but rather that against them You have the power to wage war, but not against the inhabitants of this land. And this is <b>and they will say to the inhabitant of this land</b> — and what will they say about them? What is stated at the end of the matter: \"Since HaShem was unable\" (Numbers 14:16), because the inhabitants of the land are strong and mighty, and Pharaoh is not comparable to thirty-one kings — the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 14:16). If so, <b>to the inhabitant</b> would mean like \"concerning the inhabitant,\" and there are many such instances. But in my opinion, the intention of Moses our master in this argument is not because the Canaanites are strong and mighty in war and Pharaoh is not comparable to thirty-one kings, for HaShem struck Egypt with pestilence and killed their firstborn in an instant, and brought upon them great plagues from heaven, and this is slight in the eyes of HaShem — to strike the mighty and the strong just like the lowly and the weak. Rather, the intention in this prayer is to say: Egypt will reckon and say that there is power in the god of Canaan to deliver from Your hand, for You executed judgments upon Egypt and upon its gods until You brought this people out from among it, but You were unable to do likewise against Canaan and against its gods. And behold, in this there would be a profanation of HaShem's name, and the hands of idol worshippers would be strengthened. And if so, it is possible that <b>to the inhabitant of this land</b> is to be taken literally — that the Egyptians would say to the people of the land of Canaan, \"Since HaShem was unable,\" etc., for in you there is a god that is nothing but divinity."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "In the final clause, the phrase כי בכם אל אפס אלהים is rendered hyper-literally; the construction is elliptical and textually difficult, apparently meaning that the Egyptians will attribute Israel's destruction to the superiority of Canaan's god."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:05:35.331903287Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}